adjudication.rationale:
In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff introduces:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar")
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array, `mainHostDestinationCalendar` becomes `undefined`. Dereferencing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will then throw a `TypeError`. This can happen in the exact scenario the comment describes (“Google Meet is selected w/o a Google Cal”), where `destinationCalendar` may be missing/empty.

In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff changes the Meet fallback check to:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  evt["location"] = "integrations:daily";
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array (which the diff increasingly uses, e.g. `destinationCalendar: []` in the webhook test), destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`. Dereferencing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will then throw a runtime TypeError. This is a real defect introduced by the diff; optional chaining (e.g. `mainHostDestinationCalendar?.integration`) or a guard is needed.
